UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 16, Lesson 1: Elements of dance: body, space and time', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Dance is made from elements. Body is what moves, such as arms, legs and torso, and the shapes you make. Space is where you move: high, middle or low levels, big or small movements and directions like forward, backward and sideways. Time is when and how fast you move, including beat, tempo and pauses. Energy is how you move: smooth, sharp, heavy or light. Dancers choose these elements to create a mood. Dance in a clear space with safe footwear, and stop if anything hurts.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nA dance shows a snail crossing a path. Choose a body shape, a level, a speed and an energy for it.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Body: curled round like a shell. Level: low near the floor. Time: very slow. Energy: smooth and heavy.'' These choices match the snail, whereas a high, quick and sharp movement would look like a different creature.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking that dance is only about steps. Dance uses all of the elements, and changing one element, such as the energy or level, can change the whole meaning.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nChoose three things: a robot, a feather and a storm. For each write a body shape, a space choice, a speed and an energy. Try each with movement in a clear space or describe it with a drawing, and tell someone what changes between them.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Creative Arts","learning_intention":"Dance is made from elements. Body is what moves, such as arms, legs and torso, and the shapes you make. Space is where you move: high, middle or low levels, big or small movements and directions like forward, backward and sideways. Time is when and how fast you move, including beat, tempo and pauses. Energy is how you move: smooth, sharp, heavy or light. Dancers choose these elements to create a mood. Dance in a clear space with safe footwear, and stop if anything hurts.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Explore and learn","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["CA2-DAN-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Choose three things: a robot, a feather and a storm. For each write a body shape, a space choice, a speed and an energy. Try each with movement in a clear space or describe it with a drawing, and tell someone what change Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using body on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Thinking that dance is only about steps.. Correction: Strong answer: ''Body: curled round like a shell.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking that dance is only about steps. Dance uses all of the elements, and changing one element, such as the energy or level, can change the whole meaning. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating body on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with body, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about body with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for body. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for body. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Thinking that dance is only about steps.","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material.","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson.","''Body: curled round like a shell."],"question":"A dance shows a snail crossing a path. Choose a body shape, a level, a speed and an energy for it. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for body; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["''Body: curled round like a shell.","Careful use of space and explain why it matters with clear evidence.","Thinking that dance is only about steps.","Dance is made from elements."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with body?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named space and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Thinking that dance is only about steps.","Name space and explain why it matters only, without linking it to body.","Apply body to new material and name the space and explain why it matters used."],"question":"Which response best applies body with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson on body. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance.","Thinking that dance is only about steps.","''Body: curled round like a shell."],"question":"Which response best uses elements, techniques and audience effect for body?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance.","Thinking that dance is only about steps.","Name a specific element or technique and say what effect it creates for body.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named."],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about body?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Example: a dance about a leaf blowing in the wind.","Thinking that dance is only about steps.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Dance is made from elements. — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name the artistic choice, its effect, and keep cultural protocols respectful.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue."],"question":"Which action keeps elements, techniques and audience effect accurate for body?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name space and explain why it matters only, with no linked example.","Choose three things: a robot, a feather and a storm.","Retype the worked example with no new example.","Thinking that dance is only about steps."],"question":"Which independent response best applies body to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about body with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating body on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using body on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for body. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with body, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Thinking that dance is only about steps.. Correction: Strong answer: ''Body: curled round like a shell.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-arts-y3-w16-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Creative Arts alignment using catalogue outcome codes (CA2-*); content-point mapping remains pending. First Nations arts content requires local Aboriginal educator or Custodian guidance before acceptance; do not invent sacred designs.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise body.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Dance","success_criteria":["I can use body with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify space and explain why it matters.","I can practise time independently on a new prompt.","I can notice energy needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Dance is made from elements.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Dance is made from elements."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about body with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: a dance about a leaf blowing in the wind. Body: arms curve and a spin. Space: moves from low to high, and travels sideways across the room. Time: a slow start, then faster, then a stop. Energy: light and floaty. The elements together show the leaf blowing away. If the energy changed to heavy and sharp, it would look like a rock.\n\nTRY FIRST\nA dance shows a snail crossing a path. Choose a body shape, a level, a speed and an energy for it.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Body: curled round like a shell. Level: low near the floor. Time: very slow. Energy: smooth and heavy.'' These choices match the snail, whereas a high, quick and sharp movement would look like a different creature.","explain":"Dance is made from elements. Body is what moves, such as arms, legs and torso, and the shapes you make. Space is where you move: high, middle or low levels, big or small movements and directions like forward, backward and sideways. Time is when and how fast you move, including beat, tempo and pauses. Energy is how you move: smooth, sharp, heavy or light. Dancers choose these elements to create a mood. Dance in a clear space with safe footwear, and stop if anything hurts.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking that dance is only about steps. Dance uses all of the elements, and changing one element, such as the energy or level, can change the whole meaning. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Body and space.","caption":"What moves and where.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Body</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">arms curve, spin</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Space</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">low to high, sideways</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: a dance about a leaf blowing in the wind. Body: arms curve and a spin. Space: moves from low to high, and trave Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: a dance about a leaf blowing in the wind. Body: arms curve and a spin. Space: moves from low to high, and trave Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. Apply the lesson idea with evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.","A guess with no artistic reason.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about body with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: a dance about a leaf blowing in the wind. Body: arms curve and a spin. Space: moves from low to high, and travels sideways across the room. Time: a slow start, then faster, then a stop. Energy: light and floaty. The elements together show the leaf blowing away. If the energy changed to heavy and sharp, it would look like a rock. Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"Time and energy.","caption":"When and how.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Time</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">slow then fast</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Energy</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">light and floaty</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison.","options":["Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about body with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking that dance is only about steps. Dance uses all of the elements, and changing one element, such as the energy or level, can change the whole meaning. Here is a more helpful example: Example: a dance about a leaf blowing in the wind. Body: arms curve and a spin. Space: moves from low to high, and travels sideways across the room. Time: a slow start, then faster, then a stop. Energy: light and floaty. The elements together show the leaf blowing away. If the energy changed to heavy and sharp, it would look like a rock.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Body: curled round like a shell. Level: low near the floor. Time: very slow. Energy: smooth and heavy.'' These choices match the snail, whereas a high, quick and sharp movement would look like a different creature.","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. Thinking that dance is only about steps. Dance uses all of the elements, and changing one element, such as the energy or level, can change the whole meaning. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about body with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nDance is made from elements. Body is what moves, such as arms, legs and torso, and the shapes you make. Space is where you move: high, middle or low levels, big or small movements and directions like forward, backward and sideways. Time is when and how fast you move, including beat, tempo and pauses. Energy is how you move: smooth, sharp, heavy or light. Dancers choose these elements to create a mood. Dance in a clear space with safe footwear, and stop if anything hurts. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new artwork or performance. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Transfer to a new artwork or performance"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 16, Lesson 1: Elements of dance: body, space and time","unit_number":4,"unit_title":"Elements of dance and movement","week":16,"week_in_unit":1,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: a dance about a leaf blowing in the wind. Body: arms curve and a spin. Space: moves from low to high, and travels sideways across the room. Time: a slow start, then faster, then a stop. Energy: light and floaty. The elements together show the leaf blowing away. If the energy changed to heavy and sharp, it would look like a rock.\n\nWrite what makes this example effective, naming the element, technique or performance choice and its effect.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Body and space.","caption":"What moves and where.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Body</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">arms curve, spin</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Space</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">low to high, sideways</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Time and energy.","caption":"When and how.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Time</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">slow then fast</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Energy</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">light and floaty</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-arts-y3-w16-l1' AND family_id IS NULL;
